  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how fluid slip at a boundary changes the way suspended particles distribute near a channel wall. In both sketches, streamlines bend around a particle-rich region on the left and then continue into a more uniformly populated suspension on the right. The key difference is the “slip length” at the wall.\n\nIn the top panel, where slip length is positive, particles are shown across nearly the full channel height, including close to the walls. The flow lines extend right up to the boundaries, suggesting little or no particle-free layer there.\n\nIn the bottom panel, with zero slip length, the particles are confined to a narrower band in the middle of the channel. Dashed red lines mark particle-free regions near the top and bottom walls, labeled as a cell-free layer (CFL). The streamlines again curve around the concentrated suspension but now leave clear gaps adjacent to the walls.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates that wall slip appears to suppress or reduce the cell-free layer and allows particles or cells to occupy regions closer to the boundary, whereas no-slip conditions produce a pronounced near-wall depletion zone.",
